## 2. The five hard checks

### HC1 — Is the new §7 acceptance condition legitimate, or a lowered bar?

**Direct answer: the replacement is legitimate. It is not lowering the bar to make the
record passable.**

Reasoning:

1. The original negative half ("find NO consumer deriving the result from an AST type or
   printed text") was, given known live violations, unpassable *as an acceptance test for
   this record* — the record could only have "passed" by falsely asserting absence. The
   replacement converts an unprovable universal negative into a finite negative inventory
   plus a per-item obligation ("every known re-derivation is either eliminated or
   individually ruled on, each ruling recorded against the fact it affects"). That is the
   standard, methodologically sound way to handle such claims — it is the same shape as
   the duplicate-removal inventory GLM's review demanded from the repo's own
   acceptance clause.
2. The replacement is, per known item, *more* demanding, not less: each of the named
   re-derivations now requires a recorded ruling or an elimination, which is strictly more
   work than failing (or falsely passing) a universal test.
3. The record does not claim the condition currently holds. It defines the acceptance
   state (§7), declares DRAFT status, and §8 states it "has not been independently
   reviewed yet." As written, the record satisfies neither the old condition nor the new
   one (0 of 5 known re-derivations ruled on, 0 eliminated) — and it honestly says so by
   not asserting it. A bar the author concedes is unmet is not a bar lowered to make the
   record pass.

Two reservations (neither makes the replacement illegitimate):

- **Completeness of the "known" set is not established.** The record lists 4 (CORRECTION 7)
  + 1 (§6: 2689). I verified all five are real, but the record provides no documented
  negative search showing why the list is complete (no text-sniffing census; qwen's R5
  found further adjacent text checks — `endsWith("!")` at 756/1937/2820/2828, type-based
  `optionalValued` at 3394 — which the record does not mention; those are
  general-optional, not boundary facts, so this is a scope note, not a violation).
  "Every known re-derivation" is checkable only for the listed set; the completeness of
  the set itself remains an unproven claim (I found no counterexample to it; that is not
  a proof).
- **GLM's duplicate-removal inventory demand is still unmet** (see §5).

